{"name":"Poetry Mauve","hex":"#886891","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"poetry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Literature composed in verse or language exhibiting conscious attention to patterns and rhythm.","A poet's literary production."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/poetry"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Poetry","blocks":[{"text":"Poetry is a form of literary art that uses aesthetic and often rhythmic qualities of language to evoke meanings in addition to, or in place of, literal or surface-level meanings. A particular instance of poetry is a poem and is written by a poet. Poets use a variety of techniques called poetic devices, such as assonance, alliteration, consonance, euphony and cacophony, onomatopoeia, rhythm, rhyme schemes and sound symbolism, to produce musical or other artistic effects. They also frequently organize these devices into poetic structures, which may be strict or loose, conventional or invented by the poet. Poetic structures vary dramatically by language and cultural convention, but they often rely on rhythmic metre: patterns of syllable stress or syllable weight. They may also use repeating patterns of phonemes, phoneme groups, tones, words, or entire phrases. Poetic structures may even be semantic."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry","wikidata":"Q482"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABooks_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/250px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/330px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/250px-The_oldest_love_poem._S